/* book: lead: A resistance thermometer measures temperature through resistance. The RTD (resistance temperature detector) is a coil or thin film of pure metal, usually platinum; its resistance rises steadily with temperature. The thermistor is a small bead or disc of sintered ceramic oxide; its resistance falls steeply and nonlinearly. Both are passive, so a small current is passed through them and the voltage  */
/* book Figure 2.4.1: Left: the Pt100 of worked example 1 rises 0.385 Ω per degree, 119.25 Ω at 50 °C. Right: the 10 kΩ, β = 3500 K thermistor falls from 29.3 kΩ at 0 °C to 6.35 kΩ at 37 °C; in the clinical divider of worked example 2 it gives 1.961, 2.018 and 2.156 V at 35, 37 and 42 °C. */
