/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - An RTD is fine platinum wire, or a thin platinum film on ceramic, with a defined resistance at 0 °C called R₀.
   - Platinum is standard because it does not oxidise, its curve repeats from batch to batch and over years, and it is nearly linear.
   - RT is resistance at temperature T (Ω); R₀ is resistance at 0 °C (Ω); α is the temperature coefficient of resistance, 0.00385 /°C for platinum; T is temperature in °C.
   - Thus a Pt100 reads 138.5 Ω at 100 °C.
   - Callendar–Van Dusen form.
   - Connecting wires add resistance.
   - A three-wire circuit uses three equal leads and cancels one lead drop against another.
   - ΔT is temperature rise above surroundings (°C); I is measuring current (A); RT is element resistance (Ω); PD is the dissipation constant in the surroundings (W/°C).
   - That rise is self-heating.
   - A thermistor is sintered oxides of manganese, nickel, cobalt and iron, pressed into beads, discs or chips and sealed in glass or epoxy.
*/
