/* book Worked example 2: Q: A clinical probe uses the same 10 kΩ, β = 3500 K thermistor on top of a 10 kΩ fixed resistor; the output is taken across the fixed resistor. Find the output at 35, 37 and 42 °C with a 3.3 V supply and the average sensitivity. The bead’s dissipation constant is 1 mW/°C: find the self-heating at 37 °C, and repeat with a 0.5 V supply.
   1. Thermistor values from R = R₀ exp[β(1/T − 1/T₀)]: 6832 Ω at 35 °C, 6350 Ω at 37 °C, 5309 Ω at 42 °C.
   2. Vout = 3.3 × 10 000/(R + 10 000): 1.961 V at 35 °C, 2.018 V at 37 °C, 2.156 V at 42 °C.
   3. Average sensitivity = (2.156 − 1.961)/7 = 27.9 mV/°C, so a 0.1 °C step is 2.8 mV, easy to resolve.
   4. Self-heating at 37 °C: I = 3.3/16 350 = 202 µA; P = I²R = (202 × 10⁻⁶)² × 6350 = 0.259 mW; ΔT = 0.259/1 = 0.26 °C. Too large for a 0.1 °C thermometer.
   5. With 0.5 V: power scales with V², so P = 0.259 × (0.5/3.3)² = 5.9 µW and ΔT = 0.006 °C; the sensitivity falls to 27.9 × 0.5/3.3 = 4.2 mV/°C, still 0.42 mV per 0.1 °C.
   Answer: 1.961, 2.018 and 2.156 V; 27.9 mV/°C at 3.3 V, but 0.26 °C of self-heating. At 0.5 V the heating is 0.006 °C and the sensitivity 4.2 mV/°C. */
